<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 6: Data Shapes                                           -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  A short, explicitly cross-referenced     -->
<!--  callback to the Ebola district-Excel-format line already used in  -->
<!--  ch4, paired with a fresh, independently verified real stories     -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-data-shapes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Data Shapes</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-data-shapes">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> worked because every
      dataset in it was already arranged the way that a good plotting system
      expects: one row per observation, one column per variable.
      Add a variable, add an aesthetic mapping.
      That trick only works if the data is already in the right
      <term>shape</term> for it.
      Real data rarely arrives that way, and the same numbers, rearranged, can
      be either trivial or nearly unusable depending on how they are laid out.
      This chapter is about recognising that shape is a real, consequential
      property of a dataset, not a formatting detail.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: The opener, on its own, told in full                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-intro-data-shapes">
    <title>The Same Information, the Wrong Shape</title>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we told the story of the Ebola
      outbreak in West Africa from 2014 to 2016 - the largest ever recorded,
      with more than 11,000 deaths across Guinea, Liberia, and Sierra Leone.
      We focused there on the problems of data quality: duplicate records,
      missing values, inconsistent variable names, paper forms degraded by
      chlorine disinfection, data that had to be read through a fence and copied
      by hand.
      Those problems were real and serious.
      But there was a second, deeper problem running alongside all of them, and
      it is the one this chapter is about.
    </p>

    <p>
      As the outbreak grew, Sierra Leone set up District Ebola Response Centres
      (DERCs) within each district to manage the local response.
      Each centre was responsible for tracking cases and contacts in its area:
      who had tested positive, who had been in contact with them, who had died,
      what the laboratory results showed.
      This was the right structure for managing a geographically spread
      outbreak.
      Local centres could respond quickly to what was happening in their area,
      without waiting for direction from the capital.
      The data collection happened close to the ground, where the cases were.
    </p>

    <p>
      In practice, the information flowing into each DERC was substantial and
      varied.
      Case investigation forms (CIFs) were filled in by surveillance officers in
      the field and brought back to the DERC, where data managers entered them
      into the Epi Info VHF database.
      Blood samples were accompanied by a copy of the CIF to the laboratory.
      But DERCs also had to manage a continuous stream of other information on a
      daily basis: live alerts recording who had been seen and whether a sample
      had been taken; death alerts noting whether a swab had been collected and
      a burial team sent; quarantined households and their food supply; and
      confirmed EVD positive case counts from laboratory results in health
      facilities.
      Most DERCs handled this by building manual dashboards — physical displays
      showing the current situation in their district — and then transferring
      the information into separate Microsoft Excel sheets or Word documents.
    </p>

    <p>
      The result was that each DERC had data, often a great deal of it.
      But there was no agreed method for how to structure that data across
      districts.
      Each centre had their own format; for example, own column names, own
      definitions, own sense of what a row in a spreadsheet should represent.
      In one district, for example, a row might have meant a patient; in
      another, it might have meant a day, with columns recording how many new
      cases and deaths occurred on that day.
      In a third, confirmed and suspected cases might be on separate sheets
      entirely.
      None of these formats was wrong - each made sense to the team that
      designed it given the forms they had and the questions they were being
      asked locally.
      But they were incompatible with each other in ways that turned out to
      matter enormously.
    </p>

    <p>
      When the national response team in Freetown tried to combine the district
      files into a single national picture, the formats did not fit together.
      A file where a row means a patient and a file where a row means a day
      cannot simply be stacked because the rows mean different things.
      A column called <c>EVD_positive</c> in one file might or might not be the
      same as <c>confirmed_cases</c> in another.
      Before anyone could count anything at the national level, someone had to
      answer a prior question that was not agreed on before the outbreak
      started: what shape should this data be in?
    </p>

    <p>
      Owada et al.
      (2016) are direct about the consequences.
      <q>Lack of consistency</q> in how data was structured across districts
      <q>caused problems with merging and aggregating cases 
    to report critical information.</q>
      That critical information included things that sound basic: how many new
      alerts were reported today?
      How many new confirmed cases?
      How many blood tests were processed, and what did they show?
      How many deaths occurred in each district?
      These are exactly the numbers a national response team needs to see every
      day to decide where to send resources, where to open new treatment units,
      and where the outbreak is accelerating.
      Without them, decisions were being made blind, or on the basis of
      incomplete aggregations that took days of manual work to produce and were
      already out of date by the time they arrived.
    </p>

    <p>
      The deeper consequence was that much of the data proved impossible to use
      retrospectively either.
      The authors conclude that
      <q>much 
    of the data has never found its way into any structured database 
    and may never be recoverable.</q>
      These are formats that were individually reasonable and collectively
      useless at the scale the outbreak demanded.
      Data that existed, that had been collected at enormous effort under
      dangerous conditions, could not be used because no one had agreed in
      advance on what shape it should take.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two of Owada et al.'s recommendations go directly to this problem.
      The first calls for regular data harmonisation meetings between district
      monitoring and evaluation officers and national data managers — not just
      during an emergency, but before one, so that when the crisis comes, the
      formats are already agreed.
      The second calls for training of local surveillance officers and data
      clerks in how to collect and enter data in a standardised way.
      Both recommendations are, at their core, about shape: agreeing in advance
      what a row means, what a column is called, and how files from different
      places will eventually be combined.
      These are not technical niceties.
      In the Ebola response, they were the difference between data that could be
      used to fight an outbreak and data that could not.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter is about data shape: what it means, why it matters, and the
      operations that change it.
      If the district response centres had all used the same format, appending
      their files would have been trivial.
      If lab results had shared a patient identifier with case records, merging
      them would have been straightforward.
      And if there had been a shared understanding of what shape the national
      reporting needed, much of what was lost might have been recoverable.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Sources:</term> Owada, K., Eckmanns, T., Kamara, K.O.B.
      and Olu, O.O.
      (2016).
      <q>Epidemiological Data Management during an
        Outbreak of Ebola Virus Disease: Key Issues and Observations from
        Sierra Leone.</q>
      <em>Frontiers in Public Health</em>, 4, 163.
    </p>
  </section>
  
