  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: Applying and looking at different shapes              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-applied">
    <title>Shape Problems in Practice</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The next three sections work through the same shape problems in
        different datasets (flamingos, Kenya road accidents, and the census).
        The aim is to show exactly what goes wrong and how to fix it, before
        meeting an example of each problem in the wild.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-long-to-wide">
      <title>Wide or Long?</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          The same data can be arranged in fundamentally different ways
          depending on who needs to use it:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Long format</term> is one row per observation, one column
                per variable.
                This is the shape that plotting and analysis tools expect.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Wide format</term> is one column per category, one row per
                group.
                This is the shape that a human reader often wants to scan.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Knowing when to use each and how to move between them is a useful
          skills when working with data.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-shapes-long-to-wide">
        <title>Pivoting the Flamingos Data</title>

        <p>
          <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> has always been in <term>long</term>
          format: one row per bird, one column per variable.
          That shape is why all our visualisations using <c>ggplot2</c> work
          without any reshaping.
          But long format is not always what you want to <em>look</em> at, and
          reshaping between the two is a skill in its own right.
        </p>

        <p>
          Suppose you want a simple summary table, birds per lake per species,
          laid out the way a person reading a report would expect: lakes down
          the side, species across the top.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)

        counts_long &lt;- flamingos %&gt;%
          count(lake, species)

        head(counts_long)

        #&gt;          lake          species  n
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria Greater Flamingo 10
        #&gt; 2     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo 114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo 30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita  Lesser Flamingo 38
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru Greater Flamingo 26
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo 126
        </program>
        <p>
          <c>counts_long</c> is exactly the shape a computer wants: one row per
          lake-species combination, one column holding the count.
          But, it is not the best shape to communicate to a human.
          Comparing species counts across lakes is easier when the species are
          in columns instead of rows.
          <c>pivot_wider()</c> turns the <c>species</c> values into column
          names.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
        counts_wide &lt;- counts_long %&gt;%
          pivot_wider(names_from = species, values_from = n)

        counts_wide

        #&gt;          lake Greater Flamingo Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria               10             114
        #&gt; 2 Elementaita               30              38
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru               26             126
        </program>
        <p>
          Now the table is laid out in a way that is easy to read by eye, but it
          is no longer in a shape that <c>ggplot2</c> can plot directly.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>From wide to long</term>
        </p>

        <p>
          Reverse the process with <c>pivot_longer()</c>, and <c>counts_wide</c>
          becomes plottable again.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
        counts_long &lt;- counts_wide %&gt;%
          pivot_longer(cols = -lake, names_to = "species", values_to = "n")
        counts_long

        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 3
        #&gt;   lake        species              n
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Bogoria     Greater Flamingo    10
        #&gt; 2 Bogoria     Lesser Flamingo    114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo    30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Lesser Flamingo     38
        #&gt; 5 Nakuru      Greater Flamingo    26
        #&gt; 6 Nakuru      Lesser Flamingo    126

        
ggplot(counts_long, aes(x = lake, y = n, fill = species)) + 
  geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
  labs(x = "Lake", y = "Count", fill = "Species")
        </program>
        <figure>
          <caption>
            A bar chart with three bars comparing the number of flamingoes at
            each lake, split by species.
            Created using data in a long format.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch6_long_plot.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A bar chart with three bars comparing the number of flamingoes
                at each lake, split by species.
                The bar for Elementaita is the tallest, followed by Nakuru, then
                Bogoria.
                The bar for Lesser Flamingoes is taller than the bar for Greater
                Flamingoes at each lake.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          This round trip, long <mdash/> wide <mdash/> long, is not busywork.
          Each direction serves a different reader.
          Long format serves R: <c>ggplot2</c>, <c>group_by()</c>, and joins all
          expect it.
          Wide format serves a person scanning a table by eye, or a spreadsheet
          cell reference that assumes species live in fixed columns.
          Knowing which one a task actually needs, before writing any code,
          saves exactly the repeated reshaping that cost Gassen a day's work
          when JHU's columns shifted under him, and exactly the merging failure
          that cost Sierra Leone's Ebola response.
          This JHU example is given below.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="sec-jhu-shapes">
        <title>A Format That Grew a New Column Every Day</title>

        <p>
          In January 2020, a small team at Johns Hopkins University's Center for
          Systems Science and Engineering (CSSE) launched a public COVID-19
          dashboard, described the following May in a peer-reviewed paper by
          Dong, Du, and Gardner (2020).
          It became, within weeks, one of the single most-used sources of
          COVID-19 data in the world <mdash/> the map that news outlets,
          governments, and researchers everywhere pulled numbers from.
          The underlying data was published openly, and it started out
          <term>wide</term>: one row per country or region, and one column for
          every date, holding that location's cumulative case count on that day.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>A table with no final column</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          Most wide tables have a fixed, known set of columns.
          This one did not.
          Every single day the pandemic continued, the dataset needed one more
          column than it had the day before, for as long as anyone was tracking
          the outbreak.
          Anyone who wanted to do more than eyeball the day's totals <mdash/>
          plot a trend, compute a weekly change, merge in a country's population
          to calculate a rate <mdash/> first had to reshape the table from wide
          to long, and had to do it again every time a new day's column was
          added.
          Wide format was not <em>hard</em> to use.
          It was hard to use
          <em>repeatedly, at
        growing scale, indefinitely.</em>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-covid-wide-format">
          <caption>
            Confirmed COVID-19 cases worldwide, 22 January to 2 February 2020.
            Each row represents a country or region, while each date is stored
            as a separate column.
            As more days are added, the dataset quickly becomes very wide.
            This is one of three datasets in the original collection: Confirmed
            Cases, Deaths, and Recovered.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/covid_wide_format.png" width="60%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A screenshot of a wide COVID-19 dataset.
                Rows represent countries and regions, with columns for location,
                latitude and longitude followed by daily confirmed case counts
                from 22 January 2020 onwards.
                The many date columns illustrate how the dataset becomes
                increasingly wide as observations are recorded for each new day.
                This is the "Confirmed Cases" dataset; equivalent datasets were
                provided for deaths and recovered cases.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <alert>Reshaping it themselves, and having the ground move</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          For a dashboard glanced at once a day, wide was never actually broken.
          It was the perfect format.
          But, some individuals needed to do more than glance at the data, and
          so they reshaped it themselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          On 23 March 2020, the researcher Joachim Gassen published R code that
          pulled JHU's wide file and reshaped it to long <mdash/> one row per
          country per date <mdash/> specifically so it could be merged with
          other data.
          A wide table with a different date in every column has no single
          column two datasets can agree to join on; a long table does.
          Gassen's code went further, attaching standardised <term>ISO3c</term>
          country codes to every row, because without a shared identifier,
          nobody could reliably bring in a country's population to convert raw
          case counts into a per-capita rate.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-covid-long-format">
          <caption>
            The same confirmed-case numbers as
            <xref ref="fig-covid-wide-format"/>, reshaped to long format: one
            row per country per date, with a standardised country code attached
            for merging.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/covid_long_format.png" width="60%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A long-format COVID-19 dataset with one row per country per
                date.
                Columns include a standardised ISO3c country code, country name,
                date, and confirmed case count, instead of one column per date.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The reshaping held up for about a day.
          On 24 March 2020, Gassen published a follow-up post:
          <q>Just hours after my old blog post
        about tidying Johns Hopkins CSSE Covid-19 data the team has changed
        their time-series table data structure.</q>
          JHU had restructured the wide file's own columns <mdash/> adding
          latitude and longitude, and reorganising how US states were broken out
          <mdash/> a genuine improvement, but one that broke every piece of code
          written against the old column layout, Gassen's included.
          He rewrote and republished the same day.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-jhu-shapes-lesson">
          <title>
            Reshaping isn't a one-time fix.
            It's a job that keeps coming back.
          </title>

          <p>
            JHU never made its published file long, and it didn't need to
            <mdash/> wide genuinely was the right shape for a dashboard scanned
            once a day.
            The people who needed it long, to merge, to compute rates, to plot
            trends across countries, had to reshape it themselves.
            And because they did not control the source format, a change nobody
            warned them about broke that reshaping within a day of them
            publishing it.
            Wide and long are not a wrong version and a right version of the
            same table.
            They serve different readers, and whoever needs the other shape is
            the one who ends up doing the reshaping <mdash/> repeatedly, for as
            long as the source keeps changing underneath them.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Dong, E., Du, H.
          and Gardner, L.
          (2020).
          <q>An interactive web-based dashboard to track COVID-19 in real
        time.</q>
          <em>The Lancet Infectious Diseases</em>, 20(5), 533 <ndash/> 534.
          Gassen, J.
          (23 March 2020).
          <q>Tidying the John Hopkins Covid-19 data.</q> Blog post.
          Gassen, J.
          (24 March 2020).
          <q>Tidying the new Johns Hopkins Covid-19
        time-series datasets.</q>
          Blog post.
          Data repository:
          <url href="https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19" visual="github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-append">
      <title>Appending Data</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Appending is the simplest shape operation: take two datasets that
          describe the same kind of thing and stack them into one.
          It sounds straightforward, and when everything is consistent it is.
          But column names, data types, and variable definitions all need to
          match.
          When they do not, the result is either an error or, more dangerously,
          a silent expansion that looks fine until you try to use it.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-shapes-append">
        <title>Appending the 2016 and 2017 Kenya Road Accidents Data</title>

        <p>
          In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/> and
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> we worked with the Kenya Road
          Accidents database covering April to June 2016.
          This same excel file contains a second sheet with data from 2017.
          This is a natural appending problem: two datasets recording the same
          kind of information, in the same format, for different time periods.
          The goal is to combine them into one.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)
        library(readxl)

        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")
        kenya_accidents_2017 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx",
                                            sheet = "2017")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Before combining anything, it is worth looking at both datasets side
          by side to see how they compare.
          What columns do they share?
          What is different?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        head(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 13
        #&gt;   `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY       ROAD            PLACE
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI      KITUI-ITHOKWE   KITUI SCHOOL
        #&gt; 2 830             VOI             TAITA TAVETA MOMBASA-NAIROBI IKANGA
        #&gt; 3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI       MOMBASA-NAIROBI KATOLANI
        #&gt; 4 2100            ONGATA RONGAI   NAKURU       NAKURU-NAIROBI  MAASAI LODGE
        #&gt; 5 1900            MATUU           MACHAKOS     MATUU-MWINGI    KIVANI
        #&gt; 6 2130            NYANDO          HOMA BAY     KATITO-KENDU B… PAP ONDITI
        #&gt; # ... with 8 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, ...13 &lt;chr&gt;

        head(kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 15
        #&gt;   `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY  ROAD  PLACE `MV INVOLVED`
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 745             KISUMU          KISUMU  NAIR… KASA… KBS 163T/ZD …
        #&gt; 2 1430            NAROK           NAROK   NARO… NARO… KBS 518A HON…
        #&gt; 3 1515            LONDIANI        KERICHO KERI… JUBE… KTCB 472K JO…
        #&gt; 4 1600            KIMILILI        BUNGOMA BOKO… BITU… KBW 374M TOY…
        #&gt; 5 1747            NAKURU          NAKURU  PIPE… PIPE… KMDQ 433R DA…
        #&gt; 6 1830            ONGATA RONGAI   KAJIADO MAGA… CHAP… KBG 936M T/M…
        #&gt; # ... with 9 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;, AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;, Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, ...15 &lt;dttm&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The two datasets share 12 columns with the same names and the same
          data types: <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c>, <c>COUNTY</c>, <c>GENDER</c>,
          <c>AGE</c>, and so on.
          The 2016 data has a 13th column ( <c>...13</c> ) that we established
          in the cleaning chapter is just a stray note, not real data.
          The 2017 data has two extra columns that 2016 does not:
          <c>MV INVOLVED</c> (the vehicle registration number) and
          <c>NAME OF VICTIM</c>, as well as its own stray column ( <c>...15</c>
          ).
          We drop the stray columns from both before proceeding.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016$`...13` &lt;- NULL
        kenya_accidents_2017$`...15` &lt;- NULL
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          <alert>When the columns match: a clean append</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          In <c>dplyr</c>, <c>bind_rows()</c> stacks two data frames vertically:
          the rows of the second are placed below the rows of the first.
          It is the computational equivalent of printing both spreadsheets and
          stapling them together.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_all &lt;- bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                          kenya_accidents_2017)

        head(kenya_accidents_all, 10)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 10 × 14
        #&gt;    `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY       ROAD            PLACE
        #&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt;  1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI      KITUI-ITHOKWE   KITUI SCHOOL
        #&gt;  2 830             VOI             TAITA TAVETA MOMBASA-NAIROBI IKANGA
        #&gt;  3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI       MOMBASA-NAIROBI KATOLANI
        #&gt;  4 2100            ONGATA RONGAI   NAKURU       NAKURU-NAIROBI  MAASAI LODGE
        #&gt;  5 1900            MATUU           MACHAKOS     MATUU-MWINGI    KIVANI
        #&gt;  6 2130            NYANDO          HOMA BAY     KATITO-KENDU B… PAP ONDITI
        #&gt;  7 1200            MWEA            KIRINYAGA    MAKUTANO-MUMB… MUMAI
        #&gt;  8 1330            NAKURU          NAKURU       KENYA-UGANDA    NEAR NAKURU
        #&gt;  9 1930            KAYOLE          NAIROBI      EASTERN BYPASS  NEAR KAYOLE
        #&gt; 10 2030            THIKA           KIAMBU       NYERI ROAD      BLUE POSTS
        #&gt; # ... with 4 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, MV INVOLVED &lt;chr&gt;, NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          It works.
          Notice that the columns that exist only in the 2017 data (
          <c>MV INVOLVED</c> and <c>NAME OF VICTIM</c> ) appear in the combined
          dataset, filled with <c>NA</c> for all the 2016 rows.
          <c>bind_rows()</c> handles this gracefully: it creates a column for
          every column that exists in either dataset, and fills the gaps with
          missing values.
          No data is lost and nothing errors.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can verify that the append worked by plotting the date
          distribution.
          If both years are present, we should see dates spanning 2016 and 2017.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(ggplot2)
        library(lubridate)

        kenya_accidents_all %&gt;%
          mutate(year = lubridate::year(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)) %&gt;%
          count(year)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 2
        #&gt;    year     n
        #&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1  2016   378
        #&gt; 2  2017   740
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          There are 378 rows from 2016 and 740 from 2017.
          Both years present, as expected.
          This is the kind of quick check worth doing after every append:
          confirm that the row count is what you expected, and that the data
          from each source is actually there.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is also worth checking the columns.
          The 2016 data had 12 columns and the 2017 data had 14 — but the
          combined dataset does not have 12 + 14 = 26 columns.
          It has 14, because most columns are shared between the two datasets
          and appear only once.
          The two extra columns from 2017 ( <c>MV INVOLVED</c> and
          <c>NAME OF VICTIM</c> ) are added to the shared set, giving 14 in
          total.
          If a column name appeared in one dataset but not the other and was
          genuinely a different variable — not just a naming inconsistency — you
          would expect to see it appear as an extra column filled with <c>NA</c>
          s for the dataset that did not have it.
          Checking the column count after an append, and inspecting any
          unexpected columns, is a quick way to catch naming mismatches before
          they cause problems downstream.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>When the column names don't match</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          A clean append depends on both datasets using the same column names
          for the same variables.
          One issue that may have arisen in Ebola response story is if different
          districts used different names for the same field.
          In this case, the files could not be combined correctly.
          We can recreate that problem deliberately.
        </p>

        <p>
          Suppose the 2016 data had been collected with the column called
          <c>SEX</c> instead of <c>GENDER</c>.
          We simulate that by renaming it:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- dplyr::rename(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                               "SEX" = "GENDER")
        names(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt;  [1] "TIME 24 HOURS"          "BASE/SUB BASE"         
        #&gt;  [3] "COUNTY"                 "ROAD"                  
        #&gt;  [5] "PLACE"                  "BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS"
        #&gt;  [7] "SEX"                    "AGE"                   
        #&gt;  [9] "CAUSE CODE"             "VICTIM"                
        #&gt; [11] "NO."                    "Date DD/MM/YYYY"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Now what happens when we append?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 10 × 16
        #&gt;    `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY  ROAD  PLACE
        #&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt;  1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI KITU… KITU…
        #&gt;  2 830             VOI             TAITA … MOMB… IKAN…
        #&gt;  3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI  MOMB… KATO…
        #&gt; # ... with 7 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   SEX &lt;chr&gt;, AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NO. &lt;dbl&gt;, Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, MV INVOLVED &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          No error — but look carefully at the columns.
          Both <c>SEX</c> and <c>GENDER</c> appear in the combined dataset as
          separate columns.
          R has no way of knowing that <c>SEX</c> in the 2016 data and
          <c>GENDER</c> in the 2017 data are the same variable.
          From R's perspective, they are two different column names, so they
          become two different columns.
          The 2016 rows have values in <c>SEX</c> and <c>NA</c> in
          <c>GENDER</c>; the 2017 rows have values in <c>GENDER</c> and
          <c>NA</c> in <c>SEX</c>.
          The information is all there, but it is now split across two columns
          that should be one.
          Any analysis of sex or gender would need to reconcile them first.
        </p>

        <p>
          The ideal fix is to standardise the column name before appending:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- dplyr::rename(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                               "GENDER" = "SEX")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          <alert>When the column types don't match</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          A more subtle problem arises when both datasets use the same column
          name but store the values as different types.
          The date column is a good illustration.
          In both datasets, <c>Date DD/MM/YYYY</c> is read in as a
          <c>POSIXct</c> datetime object.
          But suppose we convert it to a simpler <c>Date</c> type in the 2016
          data as part of cleaning, as we did in chapter 4, while leaving the
          2017 data unconverted:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
          mutate(`Date DD/MM/YYYY` = as.Date(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`))

        class(kenya_accidents_2016$`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)
        #&gt; [1] "Date"

        class(kenya_accidents_2017$`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)
        #&gt; [1] "POSIXct" "POSIXt"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Both represent dates, but R stores them differently.
          What happens when we append?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          It works!
          <c>bind_rows()</c> silently converts both to <c>dttm</c> (datetime)
          format to resolve the conflict.
          This is convenient, but <q>silently</q> is worth noting: R made a
          decision about your data without telling you.
          In this case the decision is harmless, but in general it is worth
          checking the types of the combined dataset after appending, rather
          than assuming everything was resolved correctly.
        </p>

        <p>
          Not all type mismatches are resolved so gracefully.
          Convert <c>AGE</c> to numeric in the 2016 data, like in
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, using <c>as.numeric()</c>, and
          leave it as character in the 2017 data.
          The result is an error when you try to append:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
          mutate(AGE = as.numeric(AGE))

        class(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE)
        #&gt; [1] "numeric"

        class(kenya_accidents_2017$AGE)
        #&gt; [1] "character"

        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; Error in `bind_rows()`:
        #&gt;   ! Can't combine `..1$AGE` &lt;double&gt; and `..2$AGE` &lt;character&gt;.
        #&gt; Run `rlang::last_trace()` to see where the error occurred.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Unlike the date mismatch, R cannot automatically decide whether a
          number and a string are the same thing, so it refuses rather than
          guessing.
          This error is actually helpful: it is telling you exactly which column
          is causing the problem and what the conflict is.
          The fix is to make the types consistent before appending — either
          convert <c>AGE</c> to numeric in both datasets, or leave it as
          character in both:
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-append-lesson">
          <title>
            Appending reveals assumptions you didn't know you'd made.
          </title>

          <p>
            A clean append requires that both datasets agree on three things:
            the same column names for the same variables, the same data types
            for each column, and the same definition of what a row represents.
            When any of those assumptions breaks, the result is either an error
            (which at least tells you something went wrong) or a silent
            expansion — extra columns appearing, types being quietly coerced —
            which may not.
            The two sheets of the Kenya road accidents data fail at none of
            them, which is why the append works so cleanly.
            That is not accidental: it reflects a consistent data collection
            format applied across both years.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-binding-by-rows">
        <title>
          Binding by Rows: Stacking Farmer Lists for Fertilizer Vouchers
        </title>

        <p>
          Every planting season, the Kenyan government subsidises fertiliser for
          smallholder farmers and distributes it through National Cereals and
          Produce Board (NCPB) depots.
          A farmer registers once, and is issued a voucher by SMS: turn up at
          the depot with your phone and your ID, the clerk checks you against
          the system, and you walk away with fertiliser at a fraction of the
          market price.
          For a smallholder farmer, that price difference is not a minor saving.
          It can be the difference between planting a full acre and planting
          half of one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Behind that single lookup at the depot counter sits a chain of
          ordinary data work: registration lists have to be combined into one
          national list, and that list has to be checked against a farmer's ID
          and phone details before a voucher is issued.
        </p>

        <p>
          One way to build a national registry is to take every local list of
          registered farmers and stack them into a single table, one after
          another.
          For it to work cleanly, every list being stacked has to agree on its
          columns: the same column names, meaning the same thing, all the way
          down.
          If one office's spreadsheet calls a column <c>phone_number</c> and
          another calls the same thing <c>mobile_no</c>, software does not stop
          and ask what to do.
          It creates a new column for the name it hasn't seen before, and fills
          it with <term>NA</term> <mdash/> <q>not available</q>, the standard
          way software marks a hole in the data <mdash/> everywhere that column
          does not apply.
          A registry that should have five clean columns quietly grows extra
          ones nobody asked for, and a farmer's real phone number can end up
          split across two of them depending on which office registered them.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-schema-drift">
          <title>Computers Match Characters, Not Intent</title>

          <p>
            Two records that look identical to a human being can be completely
            different to a computer if the column names, spacing, or exact text
            differ by even a single character.
            An append needs every table to agree on its columns before you stack
            them, and ideally needs a rule for what counts as
            <q>the same real-world entity twice</q> before it lets a duplicate
            in.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-ss-shapes-definitions">
        <title>Same Column Name, Different Definition</title>

        <p>
          Suppose we wanted to append our 2016 Kenya road crash data with
          equivalent data from Uganda to compare them.
          The two datasets might have the same column names, the same data
          types, and the same apparent structure (a row per crash, columns for
          time, location, victim category, and cause), and hence
          <c>bind_rows()</c> would run without error.
          But Kenya and Uganda define a road fatality differently.
          In Kenya, the NTSA appears to record only deaths at the scene — with
          no follow-up at hospitals to determine whether injuries sustained in
          the crash later proved fatal (Muguro et al., 2020).
          Uganda, by contrast, classifies a road crash as fatal if an individual
          dies at the scene or within a year and a day of the crash due to
          injuries sustained in it (Apunyu et al., 2024).
          This means that a person who died at the scene would be counted as a
          road fatality in both countries, but a person who survived the scene
          and died in hospital six months later would be counted in Uganda and
          not in Kenya.
          The data would contain a mix of values collected under different
          rules, with no flag to tell you which is which.
          The append would succeed technically and fail analytically.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-variable-definitions">
          <title>A Variable Name Is Not a Definition</title>

          <p>
            Software checks whether column names and data types match during an
            append; it cannot check whether the underlying data-generation
            processes are comparable.
            Before combining data from different sources, ask:
            <q>are the things being combined actually measuring 
      the same reality?</q>
          </p>

          <p>
            Identical column names are not a guarantee of identical meaning.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Muguro et al.
          (2020), cited above, on Kenya NTSA fatality definition.
          Apunyu, B.
          et al.
          (2024).
          <q>Road Traffic Fatalities in Uganda.</q> <em>Urban Science</em>,
          5(1), 29.
          <url href="https://www.mdpi.com/2673-7590/5/1/29"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-merge">
      <title>Merging: Bringing Different Information Together</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Appending stacks datasets that describe the same kind of thing.
          Merging does something different: it brings together two datasets that
          describe <em>different</em> things about the same subjects.
          The Kenya road accidents data tells us where each crash happened,
          when, and who was involved.
          We saw when exploring the data that Nairobi had the most crashes, but
          Nairobi also has the largest population of any county in Kenya.
          Does that mean Nairobi's roads are the most dangerous, or simply that
          more people use them?
          The 2019 Kenya census tells us how many people live in each county.
          Together, the two datasets can answer a question that neither could
          answer alone:
          <em>which counties have 
  the most dangerous roads relative to their population?</em>
          The answer, it turns out, is not Nairobi.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-merge-census">
        <title>Loading the census data</title>

        <p>
          The 2019 Kenya Population and Housing Census county-level data is
          available directly from the Kenya National Bureau of Statistics (KNBS)
          website, and also from the Humanitarian Data Exchange (HDX).
          This is the same platform the accidents data came from.
          <url href="https://www.knbs.or.ke/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx">Download
      the county-level Excel file</url>
          and load it into R:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- read_excel(
          "2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx"
        )

        head(kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 10
        #&gt;   `Population by Sex, Househo...` ...2  ...3  ...4  ...5  ...6  ...7  ...8  ...9  ...10
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;                           &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 NA                              NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA
        #&gt; 2 NA                              Total Sex   NA    NA    Hous… NA    NA    Land… Dens…
        #&gt; 3 County                          NA    Male  Fema… Inte… Total Conv… Grou… Sq Km Pers…
        #&gt; 4 KENYA                           4756… 2354… 2401… 1524  1214… 1204… 1008… 5808… 81.8…
        #&gt; 5 MOMBASA                         1208… 6102… 5980… 30    3784… 3762… 2127  219.… 5495…
        #&gt; 6 KWALE                           8668… 4251… 4416… 18    1731… 1728… 374   8253… 105.…
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This is an interesting start.
          The spreadsheet has two rows of headers: a merged header spanning
          multiple columns on row 2, and the actual column names on row 3.
          The data doesn't start until row 4.
          R expects exactly one header row, so it has read the first row of data
          as column names and everything else as data.
          We skip the first three rows and supply our own column names:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- read_excel(
          "2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx",
          skip = 3
        )

        names(kenya_census_2019) &lt;- c("County", "Population", "Male", "Female",
                                       "Intersex", "Households",
                                       "Households_Conventional",
                                       "Households_Group_Quarters",
                                       "Land_Area_Sq_Km",
                                       "Density_Per_Sq_Km")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The last row of the file contains a source note rather than data.
          By flicking through the data we can see that Population has a value
          for all rows, except this source note row.
          So, we drop this final row by removing any row where <c>Population</c>
          is <c>NA</c>:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        tail(kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 10
        #&gt;   County       Population    Male  Female Intersex Households ...
        #&gt; 1 HOMA BAY        1131950  539560  592367       23     262036 ...
        #&gt; 2 MIGORI          1116436  536187  580214       35     240168 ...
        #&gt; 3 KISII           1266860  605784  661038       38     308054 ...
        #&gt; 4 NYAMIRA          605576  290907  314656       13     150669 ...
        #&gt; 5 NAIROBI CITY    4397073 2192452 2204376      245    1506888 ...
        #&gt; 6 Source: 201…         NA      NA      NA       NA         NA ...

        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- kenya_census_2019 %&gt;%
          dplyr::filter(!is.na(Population))
          </code>
        </program>

        <note>
          <p>
            Notice that the census data is in wide format: one row per county,
            with separate columns for male and female population.
            That is the right shape for displaying as a summary table, but as we
            discussed earlier in this chapter, we may want to reshape it later
            if we need to plot or analyse by sex.
          </p>
        </note>

        <p>
          <alert>Choosing the right join</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          Before joining, we need to tell R which column in each dataset
          contains the shared identifier.
          In <c>dplyr</c>, the join functions take a <c>by</c> argument
          specifying this.
          If we forget to supply it:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_join &lt;- dplyr::full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                           kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; Error in `full_join()`:
        #&gt;   ! `by` must be supplied when `x` and `y` have no common variables.
        #&gt;   ℹ Use `cross_join()` to perform a cross-join.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The error tells us exactly what is wrong: the two datasets share no
          column with the same name, so R cannot guess which columns to join on.
          In the accidents data the county column is called <c>COUNTY</c>; in
          the census it is called <c>County</c>.
          We specify the match explicitly:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_join &lt;- dplyr::full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                           kenya_census_2019,
                                           by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          But examining the result reveals a problem.
          Let's look at the first five rows of the joined dataset, which shows
          both the accident columns (from the accidents data) and the population
          columns (from the census):
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-joined-sample">
          <title>
            First five rows of the joined dataset, showing accident columns
            (from the accidents data) and population columns (from the census).
            Row 2 has <c>NA</c> for all census columns because
            <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> did not yet match the census name
            <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="right">
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Variable</cell>
              <cell>Row 1</cell>
              <cell>Row 2</cell>
              <cell>Row 3</cell>
              <cell>Row 4</cell>
              <cell>Row 5</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>TIME 24 HOURS</cell>
              <cell>630</cell>
              <cell>830</cell>
              <cell>1330</cell>
              <cell>2100</cell>
              <cell>1900</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>BASE/SUB BASE</cell>
              <cell>KITUI</cell>
              <cell>VOI</cell>
              <cell>MARIAKANI</cell>
              <cell>ONGATA RONGAI</cell>
              <cell>MATUU</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>MAKUENI</cell>
              <cell>TAITA TAVETA</cell>
              <cell>KILIFI</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU</cell>
              <cell>MACHAKOS</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
              <cell>KITUI-ITHOKWE</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>MATUU-MWINGI</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>PLACE</cell>
              <cell>KITUI SCHOOL</cell>
              <cell>IKANGA</cell>
              <cell>KATOLANI</cell>
              <cell>MAASAI LODGE</cell>
              <cell>KIVANDINI</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</cell>
              <cell>HEAD ON COLLISION</cell>
              <cell>HEAD ON COLLISION</cell>
              <cell>THE UNKNOWN M/V HIT THE MOTOR CYCLE</cell>
              <cell>
                THE VEHICLE KNOCKED DOWN A PEDESTRIAN WHO WAS CROSSING THE ROAD
              </cell>
              <cell>
                THE VEHICLE OVERTOOK A M/CYCLE AND LOST CONTROL VEERING OFF THE
                ROAD &amp; ROLLED
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>GENDER</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>AGE</cell>
              <cell>26</cell>
              <cell>28</cell>
              <cell>A &amp; J</cell>
              <cell>65</cell>
              <cell>A</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>CAUSE CODE</cell>
              <cell>7</cell>
              <cell>25</cell>
              <cell>98</cell>
              <cell>29</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>VICTIM</cell>
              <cell>M/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>M/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>M/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>PEDESTRIAN</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>NO.</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Date DD/MM/YYYY</cell>
              <cell>2016-06-25</cell>
              <cell>2016-06-25</cell>
              <cell>2016-06-25</cell>
              <cell>2016-06-25</cell>
              <cell>2016-06-25</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><em>Census columns below</em></cell>
              <cell/>
              <cell/>
              <cell/>
              <cell/>
              <cell/>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Population</cell>
              <cell>987,653</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>1,453,787</cell>
              <cell>2,162,202</cell>
              <cell>1,421,932</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Male</cell>
              <cell>489,691</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>704,089</cell>
              <cell>1,077,272</cell>
              <cell>710,707</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Female</cell>
              <cell>497,942</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>749,673</cell>
              <cell>1,084,835</cell>
              <cell>711,191</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Intersex</cell>
              <cell>20</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>25</cell>
              <cell>95</cell>
              <cell>34</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Households</cell>
              <cell>244,669</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>298,472</cell>
              <cell>616,046</cell>
              <cell>402,466</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Households (Conventional)</cell>
              <cell>243,979</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>297,990</cell>
              <cell>598,237</cell>
              <cell>399,523</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Households (Group Quarters)</cell>
              <cell>690</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>482</cell>
              <cell>17,809</cell>
              <cell>2,943</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Land Area (Sq Km)</cell>
              <cell>8,176.67</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>12,553.27</cell>
              <cell>7,504.91</cell>
              <cell>6,037.27</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Density (per Sq Km)</cell>
              <cell>120.79</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>115.81</cell>
              <cell>288.11</cell>
              <cell>235.53</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Row 2 of the joined dataset has <c>NA</c> for all the census columns
          because <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> did not match the census name
          <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
          In fact, there are several counties in the accidents data that did not
          match the census, and hence have <c>NA</c> for all columns.
          This has happened where the county names are recorded differently in
          the two datasets.
          We can find these by looking for crash rows where <c>Population</c> is
          <c>NA</c>:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(Population)) %&gt;%
      pull(COUNTY) %&gt;%
      unique()
    #&gt; [1] "TAITA TAVETA"    "NAIROBI"         "KERCHO"
    #&gt; [4] "MURANGA"         "MWINGI"          "ELGEYO MARAKWET"
    #&gt; [7] "MAKURU"          "NYAHURURU"        NA
    #&gt; [10] "MARAKWET"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Each of these is a county name that appeared in the accidents data but
          found no match in the census.
          Comparing these to the County names in the census data shows where the
          problems lay.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Some are spelling differences: the accidents data used
                <c>NAIROBI</c> where the census uses <c>NAIROBI CITY</c>, and
                <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> where the census uses <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Some are errors carried forward from the original data:
                <c>KERCHO</c> and <c>MAKURU</c> were identified as typos for
                <c>KERICHO</c> and <c>NAKURU</c> back in
                <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                And some are towns that were recorded as counties when they are
                not: Nyahururu is a town in Laikipia County, and Mwingi is a
                town in Kitui County.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          For every one of these mismatches, R had no way of knowing that
          <c>NAIROBI</c> and <c>NAIROBI CITY</c> refer to the same place.
          The join key must match character for character.
          A difference of one space, one slash, or one letter is enough for R to
          treat two values as completely different.
          We still have the crash and the population data, but without an
          identical key, the two cannot be connected.
          Hence, we need to change those counties names in the accidents data to
          match the census names before we can join them.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can also check whether any rows have <c>NA</c> as their county
          value.
          These are crashes where the county was not recorded at all:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(COUNTY))
          </code>
        </program>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-na-county">
          <title>
            Rows with missing county values.
            The county can be inferred from the <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column:
            Nanyuki and Kinangop are both towns, not counties.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME</cell>
              <cell>BASE/SUB BASE</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
              <cell>PLACE</cell>
              <cell>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</cell>
              <cell>GENDER</cell>
              <cell>AGE</cell>
              <cell>CAUSE CODE</cell>
              <cell>VICTIM</cell>
              <cell>NO.</cell>
              <cell>Date</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2205</cell>
              <cell>NANYUKI</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>KINAMBA</cell>
              <cell>NGARUA</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle overturned killing 6 victims</cell>
              <cell>4F &amp; INF</cell>
              <cell>A</cell>
              <cell>26</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>6</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>2142</cell>
              <cell>KINANGOP</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NJAMBINI OLKALAO</cell>
              <cell>KAMAGUTA</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle hit a pedal cyclist</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>33</cell>
              <cell>98</cell>
              <cell>P/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Both rows have a missing county, but neither is a mystery.
          The <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column tells us where each crash was reported
          from: Nanyuki is a town in Laikipia County, and Kinangop is a
          sub-county in Nyandarua County.
          We can fill in the missing county values using <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> as
          a fallback, which is already handled by the <c>case_when()</c>
          corrections applied earlier:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
  mutate(COUNTY = case_when(
    
    # Spelling and formatting corrections to match census names
    COUNTY == "TAITA TAVETA"    ~ "TAITA/TAVETA",
    COUNTY == "NAIROBI"         ~ "NAIROBI CITY",
    COUNTY == "ELGEYO MARAKWET" ~ "ELGEYO/MARAKWET",
    COUNTY == "MARAKWET"        ~ "ELGEYO/MARAKWET",
    COUNTY == "MURANGA"         ~ "MURANG'A",
    
    # Corrections carried forward from data cleaning in Chapter 4
    COUNTY == "MAKURU"          ~ "NAKURU",
    COUNTY == "KERCHO"          ~ "KERICHO",
    
    # Towns incorrectly recorded as counties
    # Nanyuki and Nyahururu are both towns in Laikipia County
    `BASE/SUB BASE` == "NANYUKI"         ~ "LAIKIPIA",
    COUNTY == "NYAHURURU"       ~ "LAIKIPIA",
    # Mwingi is a town in Kitui County
    COUNTY == "MWINGI"          ~ "KITUI",
    # Kinangop is a sub-county in Nyandarua County
    `BASE/SUB BASE` == "KINANGOP"        ~ "NYANDARUA",
    
    # Everything else stays as is
    TRUE ~ COUNTY
  ))

  # Confirm no remaining NA counties
    kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(COUNTY)) %&gt;%
      nrow()
    #&gt; [1] 0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We can also check the other direction — which census counties have no
          corresponding crashes in the accidents data:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    setdiff(unique(kenya_census_2019$County),
            unique(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY))
    #&gt; [1] "KENYA"         "TANA RIVER"    "LAMU"
    #&gt; [4] "WAJIR"         "MANDERA"       "MARSABIT"
    #&gt; [7] "ISIOLO"        "THARAKA-NITHI" "TURKANA"
    #&gt; [10] "WEST POKOT"    "SAMBURU"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This second list is expected — these are counties with no recorded
          fatal crashes in this three-month extract, which is plausible for
          sparsely populated or remote counties.
          The first list required fixing.
          After applying all the county name corrections described earlier in
          this chapter, we verify that no mismatches remain:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    setdiff(unique(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY),
            unique(kenya_census_2019$County))
    #&gt; character(0)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Every county name in the accidents data now has an exact match in the
          census.
          Only then does it make sense to run the join again.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>Choosing the right join</alert>
        </p>

        <paragraphs>
          <title>Choosing the right join: left_join</title>

          <p>
            With the county names fixed, we run the join again.
            But we still need to think about which kind of join to use.
            A <c>full_join()</c> keeps every row from both datasets:
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
      kenya_accidents_join &lt;- full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                         kenya_census_2019,
                                         by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))

      nrow(kenya_accidents_2016)
      #&gt; [1] 378

      nrow(kenya_accidents_join)
      #&gt; [1] 389
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            We have gained 11 rows.
            These are counties that exist in the census but have no recorded
            crashes in the accidents data — Tana River, Lamu, Wajir, and others.
            A <c>full_join()</c> has added them as empty rows, with <c>NA</c>
            for every accident column.
            We do not want these.
            Our question is about crash rates, and a county with no crashes in
            the accidents extract is not the same as a county with no crashes at
            all — it simply means no fatal crash was recorded there in this
            three-month period.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we want is a <c>left_join()</c>: keep every row from the left
            dataset (the accidents data), bring in matching information from the
            right dataset (the census), and discard any right-side rows that
            have no match on the left.
            In other words — every crash gets population data attached, and no
            new rows are added.
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
      kenya_accidents_join &lt;- left_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                         kenya_census_2019,
                                         by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))

      nrow(kenya_accidents_join)
      #&gt; [1] 378
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            Back to 378 rows.
            The first five rows of the joined dataset are shown below.
            Notice that row 2 — previously <c>TAITA TAVETA</c>, which had failed
            to match — now correctly shows <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c> and has a full
            set of population values.
          </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-left-joined-sample">
          <title>
            First five rows after the corrected left join.
            Every row now has population data attached.
            Row 2 previously failed to match; after correcting the county name
            to <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c> it now joins successfully.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME</cell>
              <cell>BASE</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
              <cell>PLACE</cell>
              <cell>ACCIDENT DETAILS</cell>
              <cell>GENDER</cell>
              <cell>AGE</cell>
              <cell>CAUSE</cell>
              <cell>VICTIM</cell>
              <cell>NO.</cell>
              <cell>Population</cell>
              <cell>Male</cell>
              <cell>Female</cell>
              <cell>Density</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>630</cell>
              <cell>KITUI</cell>
              <cell>MAKUENI</cell>
              <cell>KITUI-ITHOKWE</cell>
              <cell>KITUI SCHOOL</cell>
              <cell>HEAD ON COLLISION</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>26</cell>
              <cell>7</cell>
              <cell>M/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>987,653</cell>
              <cell>489,691</cell>
              <cell>497,942</cell>
              <cell>120.79</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>830</cell>
              <cell>VOI</cell>
              <cell>TAITA/TAVETA</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>IKANGA</cell>
              <cell>HEAD ON COLLISION</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>28</cell>
              <cell>25</cell>
              <cell>M/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>340,671</cell>
              <cell>173,337</cell>
              <cell>167,327</cell>
              <cell>19.86</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>3</cell>
              <cell>1330</cell>
              <cell>MARIAKANI</cell>
              <cell>KILIFI</cell>
              <cell>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>KATOLANI</cell>
              <cell>The unknown M/V hit the motor cycle</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>A &amp; J</cell>
              <cell>98</cell>
              <cell>M/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>1,453,787</cell>
              <cell>704,089</cell>
              <cell>749,673</cell>
              <cell>115.81</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>2100</cell>
              <cell>ONGATA RONGAI</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>MAASAI LODGE</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle knocked down a pedestrian</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>65</cell>
              <cell>29</cell>
              <cell>PEDESTRIAN</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2,162,202</cell>
              <cell>1,077,272</cell>
              <cell>1,084,835</cell>
              <cell>288.11</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>1900</cell>
              <cell>MATUU</cell>
              <cell>MACHAKOS</cell>
              <cell>MATUU-MWINGI</cell>
              <cell>KIVANDINI</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle overtook a M/cycle and lost control</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>A</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>1,421,932</cell>
              <cell>710,707</cell>
              <cell>711,191</cell>
              <cell>235.53</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Finally, we confirm that no crash rows are missing population data:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(Population)) %&gt;%
      nrow()
    #&gt; [1] 0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Every crash has been matched to its county's census data.
          The join is complete.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>What the merge makes possible</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          With population data attached to every crash, we can now answer a
          question that the accidents data alone could never answer: how many
          crashes occurred <em>per person</em> in each county.
          Raw crash counts favour populous counties simply because more people
          live there and travel there.
          A rate adjusts for that, and reveals which counties have genuinely
          dangerous roads rather than just more traffic.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(ggplot2)
        library(dplyr)

        crash_rate &lt;- kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
          group_by(COUNTY, Population) %&gt;%
          summarise(crashes = n(), .groups = "drop") %&gt;%
          mutate(crashes_per_100k = crashes / Population * 100000) %&gt;%
          arrange(desc(crashes_per_100k))

        ggplot(crash_rate,
               aes(x = reorder(COUNTY, crashes_per_100k),
                   y = crashes_per_100k)) +
          geom_col(fill = "#44575E") +
          geom_col(data = filter(crash_rate, COUNTY == "NAIROBI CITY"),
                   fill = "#F37021") +
          coord_flip() +
          labs(
            title = "Fatal road crashes per 100,000 people by county",
            subtitle = "Kenya, April–June 2016. Nairobi highlighted in orange.",
            x = NULL,
            y = "Crashes per 100,000 population"
          ) +
          theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch6-crash-rate">
          <caption>
            Fatal road crashes per 100,000 population by county, Kenya, April to
            June 2016.
            Nairobi City is highlighted in orange.
            Makueni leads by a substantial margin, followed by Embu.
            Nairobi, which had the most crashes in raw terms, ranks third.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch6_crash_rate.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A horizontal bar chart with county names on the y-axis and
                crashes per 100,000 population on the x-axis, ordered from
                highest to lowest rate.
                Makueni has the longest bar at just under 2.5 crashes per
                100,000, followed by Embu at around 1.7.
                Nairobi City is shown in orange and ranks third at approximately
                1.5.
                Nakuru and Kiambu follow closely.
                The bars shorten progressively down to Nandi at the bottom with
                fewer than 0.2 crashes per 100,000.
                Counties with small populations but meaningful crash counts
                appear higher in the ranking than they would in a raw count
                chart.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The story this chart tells is quite different from the raw crash
          count.
          Nairobi had the most crashes in absolute terms — 71 in this
          three-month period — and it leads any ranking based on counts alone.
          Makueni had 26 crashes and Embu had just 10.
          In a raw count chart, Embu would barely register.
          But Nairobi has a population of over 4.4 million people; Makueni has
          just under one million; and Embu has around 560,000.
          Once population is accounted for, those 10 crashes in Embu represent a
          higher rate per person than Nairobi's 71.
          Makueni's 26 crashes, spread across a smaller population still,
          produce the highest rate of all.
        </p>

        <p>
          This matters for policy.
          A government deciding where to invest in road safety on the basis of
          raw crash counts would prioritise Nairobi, Nakuru, and Kiambu (the
          most populous counties with the most crashes).
          A government working from crash rates would also prioritise Makueni
          and Embu.
          The ranking changes because the question changes: not
          <em>where do the most crashes happen?</em> but
          <em>where are people most at risk?</em> And the only reason we can ask
          the second question is because we merged in the population data.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-merge-lesson">
          <title>A join is only as good as its key</title>

          <p>
            The entire value of this merge depended on the county names matching
            perfectly between both datasets.
            A mismatch of even one character ( <c>NAIROBI</c> v
            <c>NAIROBI CITY</c>; <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> v <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c> )
            silently drops that county from the joined result.
            Without a matching key, R has no way to connect them.
            Checking join quality is the difference between a merge that worked
            and a merge that looked like it worked.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Kenya National Bureau of Statistics (2019).
          <em>2019 Kenya Population and Housing Census, Volume I:
      Population by County and Sub-County.</em>
          Nairobi: KNBS.
          Available at:
          <url href="https://www.knbs.or.ke/2019-kenya-population-and-housing-census-results/"/>.
          Kenya Road Accidents database: openAFRICA / Humanitarian Data
          Exchange,
          <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-merging-keys">
        <title>Merging and the Idea of a Key: The Homa Bay Health System</title>

        <p>
          Homa Bay County, on the shore of Lake Victoria, has a significant HIV
          burden and a well-established HIV care programme.
          Health facilities there run on electronic medical record systems, the
          same as facilities across much of the country, but a 2025 study of
          Homa Bay's digital health landscape found that its single county
          referral hospital was running not one such system, but seven, side by
          side.
          Three of those seven, all built specifically for HIV care, do share a
          common patient identifier with each other.
          The other four cover general outpatient care, inpatient stays, the
          laboratory, and faith-based providers.
          These connect to none of the HIV systems, and not reliably to each
          other either.
        </p>

        <p>
          To make that concrete: picture a hypothetical patient, built from the
          study's own findings rather than a specific case it reported.
          A woman is HIV-positive and pregnant.
          Her HIV care sits in the HIV system.
          Her antenatal visits, being outside HIV care, are logged somewhere
          else.
          If she is admitted to hospital, her inpatient stay goes somewhere else
          again.
          This is not a stretch: the study's own interviews describe exactly
          this pattern in general, with people
          <q>assigned multiple numbers for
      different purposes, i.e., at the inpatient, outpatient and antenatal
      clinics.</q>
        </p>

        <p>
          Every operation involving merging tables depends on the same quiet
          assumption: that somewhere in your data, there is a column that
          reliably means <q>this same real-world thing</q>, everywhere it
          appears.
          That column is called a <term>key</term>.
          Homa Bay's hospital records show what happens when that assumption
          holds for only part of a system.
          TODO: we saw exactly how this can go wrong in the illustrative
          example, when county names failed to match
        </p>

        <p>
          The 2025 study found that patient identifiers were manually generated
          in most of the county's EMR systems and manually allocated at first
          interaction in 97% of facilities, with no county-wide identifier
          linking a person across every system they might touch.
          Within HIV care specifically, a shared identifier does exist and does
          its job.
          Step outside HIV care, and there is no column, anywhere in the
          hospital, that reliably means <q>this same person</q> across the rest
          of the systems she might touch.
        </p>

        <p>
          The problem is that there is nothing to <term>join</term> or
          <term>merge</term> most of those correct records together on.
          Stack or link the non-HIV systems anyway, without a shared key, and
          one of two things happens.
          Either the same person is silently counted more than once across
          different systems, inflating whatever gets reported to the county or
          the ministry at the end of the month, or a clinician treating her for
          a complication during pregnancy has no way of seeing, from where they
          are sitting, that she is currently on HIV treatment at all <mdash/>
          even though that exact information exists, correctly recorded, one
          system over.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-homa-bay-no-key">
          <title>You Cannot Join What Was Never Given a Shared Key</title>

          <p>
            A join, a stack, a reshape <mdash/> every one of these operations
            assumes a key already exists linking your tables together.
            When separate systems are built independently, with no agreement in
            advance on how a person, place, or thing will be identified across
            all of them, no amount of clever code recovers that link later.
            The key has to be designed in, before the data is collected, not
            discovered afterward.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Chepkirui, M., Dellicour, S., Musuva, R., et al.
          (2025).
          Missed opportunities for digital health data use in healthcare
          decision-making: A cross-sectional digital health landscape assessment
          in Homa Bay county, Kenya.
          <foreign>PLOS Digital Health</foreign>, 4(6), e0000870.
          https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0000870
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-key-errors">
        <title>What If the Key Is Wrong? The IEBC Voter Register</title>

        <p>
          Before national elections in Kenya, the Independent Electoral and
          Boundaries Commission (IEBC) has to check its master voter register
          against other government records: identity records, to catch invalid
          or duplicate registrations, and death records, to remove voters who
          have since died.
          Checking one table against another like this is a <term>lookup</term>,
          or <term>join</term>, and the sensible key to join on is each person's
          National ID number.
          The same register also periodically needs its own duplicates removed,
          a step usually called <term>deduplication</term>: find rows that
          represent the same person entered twice, and collapse them into one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both of these jobs depend entirely on one thing: having a key that is
          both correct for every row, and unique to one person.
          Get that wrong, and a register can break in two opposite directions at
          once.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>A key that isn't actually unique inflates the numbers.</term> If
          a system ever falls back to matching people on something other than a
          clean, unique identifier (for example, a full name and date of birth
          together), that can go wrong in an easy-to-miss way.
          If ten voters share a name and date of birth, and a second table
          contains three other people with that same name and date of birth, a
          join does not pick one sensible match.
          It matches every one of the ten against every one of the three,
          producing thirty rows out of what should have been, at most, one real
          match.
          Run that same overly broad matching rule through a deduplication step
          instead, and the failure runs the other way: anyone who happens to
          share those same details with someone else gets treated as a duplicate
          of them, and is quietly deleted, even though they are two entirely
          different, entirely real people.
        </p>

        <p>
          We saw a benign version of this in our own join.
          The <c>NAIROBI CITY</c> row appears exactly once in the 2019 census
          data.
          But it appears 71 times in the accidents data, once for each crash
          recorded in Nairobi.
          The left join correctly matched each of those 71 crash rows to the
          same single census row, repeating Nairobi's population figure 71 times
          in the joined dataset.
          That is exactly what we wanted.
          But it would be a serious problem if that same behaviour of one record
          matching many occurred in a voter register context.
          Whether repeated matches are correct or catastrophic depends entirely
          on what the key represents and what the join is meant to do.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>A key that's untidy hides real matches.</term> Even with a
          clean, unique identifier as the key, small formatting differences
          break things: one system stores an ID as a plain number, another
          stores the same ID as text with a leading zero kept in front of it, a
          third has a stray space typed in at the end of the number by accident.
          To a human, these are obviously the same ID.
          To a join or a deduplication step, they are three unrelated pieces of
          text, and no match is ever found.
        </p>

        <p>
          What KPMG's 2022 audit of the IEBC register actually reported was a
          third variant of the same underlying problem: a shared, empty value.
          The audit found that <term>481,711</term> individuals were registered
          as voters with duplicated blank ID or passport reference fields
          <mdash/> records where that field had simply never been filled in.
          A blank matches a blank exactly as confidently as a real ID matches a
          real ID, so any process checking for duplicate identity references
          will flag every one of those empty-field records against every other
          one, whether or not the people behind them are actually the same.
          The same 2022 audit also found nearly 246,000 deceased voters still
          listed as active on the register, and 226,000 entries attached to
          invalid ID numbers.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-cartesian-explosion">
          <title>A Key Is Either Too Broad or Too Untidy, Rarely Neither</title>

          <p>
            A join should never make your row count bigger than expected; if it
            does, the key you joined on is not unique on at least one side.
            A deduplication step should only ever remove rows you can point to
            as genuine duplicates; if entries you know are distinct disappear,
            your key was too broad.
            And if a join or a deduplication misses matches that are obviously
            the same to a human eye, check spacing, leading zeros, and whether
            both sides are storing the value as the same kind of thing, before
            you trust the result.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> KPMG.
          (2022).
          <foreign>Preliminary audit report on the Register of
      Voters</foreign>,
          prepared for the Independent Electoral and Boundaries Commission
          (IEBC) [As reported in: IEBC press briefing, June 8, 2022; see
          Ministry of Interior and National Administration press archive and
          contemporaneous coverage in the <foreign>Daily Nation</foreign>,
          <foreign>The Star</foreign>, and <foreign>Citizen Digital</foreign> ].
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-data-shapes">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-jhu-daily">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="insight-jhu-shapes-lesson"/> says a new column had to be
          added to the wide-format dataset every single day.
          Sketch, in words, what would have gone wrong for a downstream user's
          code if it referenced date columns by a fixed position (for example,
          <q>column 47</q> ) rather than by name.
          Would switching to long format on its own have fixed that particular
          problem?
          Why or why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-reshape-census-data">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The census data loaded in <xref ref="ss-merge-census"/> has separate
          columns for <c>Male</c> and <c>Female</c> population.
          Using <c>pivot_longer()</c>, reshape it so that each county appears
          twice, once for male population and once for female, with a new column
          called <c>sex</c> and a column called <c>population</c> holding the
          count.
          Then plot the population of males and females side by side for each
          county using <c>ggplot2</c>, with a different colour for each sex.
          Which counties have the most unequal sex distributions?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-ebola-standard">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-intro-data-shapes"/> argues that agreeing on a shape in
          advance is cheaper than reconciling incompatible shapes afterward.
          Propose one concrete rule Sierra Leone's district response centres
          could have agreed to in advance <mdash/> about column names, row
          structure, or file format <mdash/> that would have prevented the
          merging problem described in <xref ref="ss-ebola-transmission"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-flamingo-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="ss-shapes-long-to-wide"/> as a model, build a wide
          table of mean <c>body_mass_g</c> by <c>lake</c> (rows) and
          <c>species</c> (columns), instead of counts.
          Which version, this one or "table-lake-species-crosstab", would you
          send to someone who wanted to read it in an email, and which would you
          hand to someone who wanted to plot it in <c>ggplot2</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-gapminder-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>gapminder</c> package from
          <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> is in long format:
          one row per country per year.
          Using <c>pivot_wider()</c>, build a table with one row per country and
          one column per year, holding <c>lifeExp</c>.
          Try plotting directly from this wide version with the same
          <c>ggplot2</c> code from <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d"/>.
          What breaks, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>